taying at a hotel  at 1,100 Pennsylvania Avenue influence  the people at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue  it would be tough to find a specific  quid pro quo on that  but this is the type of conflict that  the Trump administration is just totally  riddled with it's kind of like a  pilgrimage right you want favorable  rulings or influence or commentary from  the Trump administration first thing you  do is you reserve some Suites at Trump's  hotel a couple of blocks down the road  from the White House and patronizing  Donald Trump's private business is now  sort of a proxy to influencing public  policy from this administration this  hotel is now the center of a bunch of  different problems you've got the  emoluments clause related to the foreign  dignitaries that stay there there's the  American executives staying there  presumably to curry some favor for  mergers and then there's the kind of  broader concern which is that the hotel  this particular hotel happens to be in a  federally owned building Donald Trump's  private business leases the building  from the federal government Trump also  happens to be the current President of  the United States so the president has a  private business from which he makes  money in a building which he rents from  the federal government which he runs and  it's important to be really transparent  this is not unique to the Trump  administration it's particularly bad  under the Trump administration but think  back to George W Bush and the Iraq war  using Halliburton to rebuild Iraq and  then of course they have Vice President  Dick Cheney who happened to have  previously been the CEO of Halliburton  involved in deciding that Halliburton  is going to be getting paid handsomely  for rebuilding Iraq that's just one  example and there are many as far as the  Trump Hotel games at some point in time  we might simply have said this is  bribery right this is just bribery  period remember when Jimmy Carter was  president he had to relinquish ownership 